 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in 3D Gaussian Splatting evolved from the need to refine initial sparse reconstructions into dense, high-quality representations. Early methods relied on static SfM points, but fine-tuning allowed for dynamic densification and optimization [S3]. The concept of per-scene optimization was adapted from NeRF methods, where a global model is fine-tuned on a specific scene to improve quality [S1]. Multi-modal fine-tuning was introduced to handle complex volumetric data with varying visual modes [S4, unofficial]. Style transfer methods explored applying fine-tuning to artistic domains, comparing baselines like Ada-GS which trains directly on 2D stylized images against methods that optimize 3D structure directly [S5].
